%I A347914 #21 Sep 01 2026 15:53:30
%S A347914 1,2,16,400,28544,5531648,2818499584,3690422056960,12207829942992896,
%T A347914 100693382983563149312,2049486901435899481686016
%N A347914 Number of quasitransitive relations on n labeled nodes.
%C A347914 Quasitransitivity is weaker than transitivity.
%C A347914 Every quasitransitive relation R is uniquely determined by its asymmetric part P, which is a strict partial order. For each unordered pair incomparable in P, either both directed pairs belong to R or neither does; the n loops are arbitrary. This gives 2^(n+inc(P)) relations with asymmetric part P. - _Vladeta Jovovic_, Sep 01 2026
%H A347914 G. Pfeiffer, <a href="https://cs.uwaterloo.ca/journals/JIS/VOL7/Pfeiffer/pfeiffer6.html">Counting Transitive Relations</a>, Journal of Integer Sequences, Vol. 7 (2004), Article 04.3.2.
%H A347914 H. J. Prömel, A. Steger and A. Taraz, <a href="https://doi.org/10.1017/S0963548301004503">Counting Partial Orders with a Fixed Number of Comparable Pairs</a>, Combinatorics, Probability and Computing, Vol. 10 (2001), 159-177.
%H A347914 Wikipedia, <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Quasitransitive_relation">Quasitransitive relation</a>
%F A347914 a(n) = 2^n * Sum_{P} 2^inc(P), where the sum is over all partial orders P on the labeled set [n], and inc(P) is the number of unordered incomparable pairs of P. - _Vladeta Jovovic_, Sep 01 2026
%e A347914 For n=2, all 2^4 = 16 binary relations are quasitransitive.
%o A347914 (SageMath)
%o A347914 def a(n):
%o A347914     s = 0
%o A347914     for P in Posets(n):
%o A347914         c = P.relations_number() - n
%o A347914         aut = P.hasse_diagram().automorphism_group().cardinality()
%o A347914         s += factorial(n)//aut * 2^(binomial(n,2) - c)
%o A347914     return 2^n * s
%o A347914 # _Vladeta Jovovic_, Sep 01 2026
%Y A347914 Cf. A006905 (number of transitive relations on n labeled nodes), A001035 (labeled partial orders), A000112 (unlabeled partial orders).
%K A347914 nonn,hard,more,changed
%O A347914 0,2
